menu "Spotify Car Thing board options"
	depends on MESON_G12A

config CARTHING_DEBUG_CONSOLE
	bool "Verbose on-screen debug console"
	depends on VIDEO
	select CONSOLE_MUX
	select EVENT
	select LINUX_KERNEL_IMAGE_HEADER
	help
	  Build a diagnostic image for units that bootloop with no UART
	  attached. Everything U-Boot prints — including pre-relocation
	  output replayed out of the pre-console buffer — is mirrored to
	  the LCD instead of just the serial port.

	  Concretely this changes:
	    - the compiled-in stdout/stderr become "serial,vidconsole",
	      and an EVT_SETTINGS_R spy re-forces them after the saved
	      uboot.env is loaded (so a unit whose saved env says
	      stdout=serial still gets the on-screen console);
	    - the panel is therefore brought up during console_init_r
	      rather than misc_init_r, early enough to catch the banner,
	      DRAM/board info and driver-model probe errors;
	    - the boot splash is suppressed (a logo painted over the log
	      helps nobody) and the backlight goes straight to full;
	    - misc_init_r prints a boot report (boot source, reboot
	      reason, env validity, A/B slot state, partition table);
	    - every path that would silently reset — in particular the
	      A/B selector's failed-boot and slot-exhausted resets, which
	      are the usual shape of a bootloop — halts instead, leaving
	      the log on screen and fastboot up for a host to attach to.
	      A bootloop otherwise destroys its own evidence.

	  Also adds the `debugreport` command so the report can be re-run
	  on demand rather than only at boot.

	  LINUX_KERNEL_IMAGE_HEADER is selected so the resulting u-boot.bin
	  carries an arm64 Linux Image header. That is what lets a unit
	  running an *unmodified, already-installed* u-boot chainload this
	  image over fastboot with `booti` — see doc/board/amlogic/
	  spotify-carthing-debug.rst. The header replaces the plain
	  `b reset` at the entry point, so the normal BL2/FIP boot path is
	  unaffected.

	  This is not meant to ship. Use spotify_carthing_defconfig for
	  normal builds.

config CARTHING_DEBUG_TRANSIENT
	bool "Treat this image as a visitor (RAM-loaded / chainloaded)"
	depends on CARTHING_DEBUG_CONSOLE
	default y
	help
	  Controls whether the image is allowed to write to the unit it is
	  diagnosing. Both variants halt on a failed boot rather than
	  resetting — that is the point of the build — so this only governs
	  persistence.

	  Say y for an image that is RAM-loaded via mask-ROM or chainloaded
	  over fastboot, i.e. one that runs once and leaves. ab_boot's
	  env_save() is suppressed, so try counters keep their values (they
	  are the evidence we came to read) and the panel console never
	  lands in uboot.env.

	  Say n for an image meant to be flashed to boot0/boot1. That image
	  is the bootloader, so the A/B state machine has to keep real
	  books: tries decrement, slots fail over, and each step is saved
	  before the halt. Progress then happens one power cycle at a time
	  instead of by self-reset, which is what makes the failure
	  photographable. Rollback still reaches the other slot. The saved
	  env is kept clean of the panel console either way, so flashing
	  back to a normal build leaves no trace.

	  spotify_carthing_debug_defconfig has this y;
	  spotify_carthing_debug_flash_defconfig has it n.

config CARTHING_AUTOPROBE
	bool "Run the hardware probe automatically at boot"
	depends on CARTHING_DEBUG_CONSOLE
	help
	  Build a single-purpose diagnostic image: at boot it runs the
	  `probe` command by itself — eMMC identity, rail voltages, A/B
	  state, a sustained 16 MiB read at each bus speed, and the rx
	  phase x delay window at DDR52 — then waits in fastboot with the
	  report held in RAM.

	  The owner of a bootlooping unit then needs two commands and no
	  understanding of any of this:

	    (chainload or ramboot the image)
	    fastboot fetch probe report.txt

	  Nothing is written to the device; the report is served from RAM
	  through a virtual fetch target. Implies TRANSIENT, since an
	  image that probes on every boot has no business being flashed.

config CARTHING_MMC_SLOW_BOOT
	bool "Pin the eMMC to a conservative speed for the boot path"
	depends on CARTHING_DEBUG_CONSOLE
	select MMC_SPEED_MODE_SET
	select DM_EVENT
	default y
	help
	  Run the eMMC in a slow single-data-rate mode while booting, and
	  lift the restriction when a host session (fastboot, UMS) starts.

	  Field units bootloop because the eMMC is marginal at DDR52 — aged
	  flash, a cold-solder BGA joint, or a weak USB supply sagging the
	  rails during init. The giveaway is a partition scan that comes up
	  empty on cold boot, populates after `mmc rescan`, and then still
	  fails to boot: a GPT read is two sectors and survives, a 22 MB
	  kernel load does not. Confirmed in the field — a unit that never
	  booted came up reliably once the bus was slowed.

	  Booting slow costs no measurable time. Flashing slow does, so the
	  pin is lifted for fastboot/UMS, where a host is attached and a
	  failure is visible and retryable. The DT keeps its full DDR52 and
	  HS200 capabilities — a mode can only be selected at runtime if the
	  DT enables it — so this restricts rather than removes.

	  `emmcspeed` shows and overrides the mode at runtime.

config CARTHING_MMC_BOOT_MODE
	int "Bus mode to pin while booting (enum bus_mode index)"
	default 1
	help
	  Deliberately not "depends on CARTHING_MMC_SLOW_BOOT": cmd_emmcspeed.c
	  is built into every image so `emmcspeed slow` always works, and the
	  IS_ENABLED() guards elsewhere still compile both arms. The symbol
	  therefore has to have a value even when the boot-time pin is off.

	  Index into enum bus_mode in include/mmc.h. 1 = MMC_HS (26 MHz
	  single-data-rate), the first step down from DDR52 and the one
	  proven in the field. Others: 3 = MMC_HS_52, 4 = MMC_DDR_52,
	  10 = MMC_HS_200. 0 = MMC_LEGACY is slower still, for a card that
	  cannot manage 26 MHz.

endmenu

# TEXT_OFFSET in the emitted arm64 Image header is
# CONFIG_TEXT_BASE - CONFIG_LNX_KRNL_IMG_TEXT_OFFSET_BASE, and the symbol
# has no default of its own (the link fails outright without one). Base 0
# makes TEXT_OFFSET equal TEXT_BASE, i.e. the header honestly says "load
# me at RAM base + 0x01000000", which is where this u-boot is linked to
# run and where booti therefore has to leave it.
config LNX_KRNL_IMG_TEXT_OFFSET_BASE
	default 0x0 if CARTHING_DEBUG_CONSOLE
